% The Department for Education's 16 to 19 funding calculation for 2026 to 2027,
% written in Logical English from the published rule documents: every element
% of section 4 of the specification (pages 37-89).
%
% Source: Department for Education, "16 to 19 funding calculation for 2026 to
% 2027: technical specification", version 1.00, September 2026,
% https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/technical-specification-further-education-for-young-people
% (a copy is beside this program). The rules there are the rule documents of
% an Oracle Intelligent Advisor (formerly Oracle Policy Automation) rulebase,
% printed as a PDF. Each rule below names the page it was written from.
%
% Contains public sector information licensed under the Open Government
% Licence v3.0 (https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/doc/open-government-licence/version/3/).
% This program is an adaptation of that information. It is not published or
% endorsed by the Department for Education. It is provided "as is", without
% warranty, and is not advice.
%
% HOW IT WAS MADE. The Department publishes the rules as a PDF, not as a Policy
% Modeling project, and publishes no test cases with the rulebase's answers.
% So this program was NOT made by the Oracle Intelligent Advisor translator,
% and it is NOT checked against Oracle Intelligent Advisor. It was written by
% hand (with an AI agent) from the printed rules. A second, independent reading
% of the same pages, written in Python by another agent that never saw this
% program, was run on the same test cases, and every difference between the
% two readings was settled against the PDF (README.md says how).
%
% HOW TO READ IT.
% - The data are the records of the specification's interface agreement
%   (pages 20-36): the provider, the postcode specialist resource reference
%   data, the learner, the learner's learning deliveries (aims), and each
%   delivery's funding and monitoring records (FAMs), LARS categories, LARS
%   validities, LARS programme funding records and hours records. A value that
%   is not stated is what the rulebase calls "unknown".
% - A rule table of the rulebase, whose rows are tried from the top, is an
%   `otherwise` cascade here, with its rows in the same order.
% - Where the rulebase concludes "uncertain", a rule here concludes the word
%   uncertain into a value marked "as computed", and the value itself is then
%   not concluded at all: "is certain" in the rulebase is "has a value" here.
% - ToUpper() and "contains" are two small Prolog goals (the upper case of a
%   text, a text contains a text); everything else is Logical English.
%
% WHAT IS READ WITH A CHOICE (README.md lists them with the reasons): the
% "both" group at the top of page 41 is read as joined by "or" to the groups
% after it, as on page 42; the row of ZTPR0012 on page 86 is read with its last
% condition inside the "for at least one" above it, as in the rows before it; a
% TemporalFromRange and ValueAt pair (pages 62 and 81) is read as the value of
% the record in force on the first day of the funding year.

the target language is: prolog.

the knowledge base dfe_16_19_funding includes these resources:
    temporal, dfe_text.pl.

the templates are:

% ---------------------------------------------------------------- the provider (pages 21-24)

the provider's retention factor is *a number*; undefined.
the provider's programme weighting is *a number*; undefined.
